# 40_spads.R
# Number of Special Political Advisers (SPADs) in UK government, 1979–2025
#
# Analytical purpose (Ch3 — The Political Class):
#   Illustrates the long-run growth in the SPAD class as evidence of
#   professionalisation and the rise of the political advisor. Not a
#   post-2014 breakpoint argument — trend substantially predates 2014.
#   Supports causal plausibility framing: the modern political class is
#   structurally different from its predecessors, amplifying turbulence
#   even if it did not cause the 2014 break.
#
# DATA SOURCES:
#   Pre-2010 (estimated): Yong & Hazell (2014) Special Advisers: Who They
#     Are, What They Do and Why They Matter; IfG Special Advisers explainer.
#     Shown with open symbols — approximate.
#   2010–2013: Cabinet Office CSV releases (individual-level name lists),
#     row-counted: June 2010 = 66, September 2012 = 83, October 2013 = 108.
#   2015–2022: Annual Report on Special Advisers (stated figures).
#   2024–2025: Cabinet Office / IfG.
#
#   DATA FILE: data/raw/spads_DATA_ENTRY.csv
#
# OUTPUT:
#   figures/ch3_spads.png

